        "title": "Enlightenment Historiography: Reason, Progress, and Criticism",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Enlightenment historiography (roughly 1680-1800) broke from Renaissance humanism and the chronicle tradition in three ways: it applied philosophical skepticism to historical sources, it substituted civilization for rulers and battles as the subject matter, and it embedded history within a master narrative of progress. The Enlightenment also produced its own critics (Vico, Herder) who challenged the universalism of the progress narrative before it had fully consolidated."
    },
